<% content_for :title, "How to use Remindly — free spoken reminders for seniors" %>
<% content_for :description, "A short guide to Remindly: how signing in works, setting up spoken reminders, reminders delivered as a phone call for anyone without a screen, the notifications caregivers get, tasks, and how caregivers and care receivers connect." %>

<h1>How to use Remindly</h1>

<p class="lead">
  Remindly speaks reminders aloud at the right time for the person being cared for, and lets the
  people who care for them set those reminders up from anywhere — on a screen at
  their end, or as a telephone call if there is no screen. Here's how the
  main pieces fit together.
</p>

<p>
  There is no password to remember — you sign in by entering your email and
  following a secure link we send you, which expires after a short time. The
  first time you sign in, Remindly asks whether you are a caregiver setting things
  up for someone or the person who will receive the reminders, and takes you
  straight in. You can change that choice later from your profile.
</p>

<h2>Reminders</h2>
<p>
  Reminders help care receivers stay on track with their daily routines. Caregivers can
  set up recurring reminders for medication, hydration, and everyday routines.
  Each reminder can be customized with:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Custom schedules</strong> — daily, weekly, or a frequency you choose.</li>
  <li><strong>Specific times</strong> — set the exact time for each reminder.</li>
  <li><strong>Categories</strong> — organize by medication, hydration, or routine.</li>
  <li><strong>Voice announcements</strong> — care receivers hear the reminder spoken aloud on their device, or over the telephone if they would rather not use a screen.</li>
  <li><strong>Acknowledgement</strong> — the care receiver marks a reminder done or snoozes it, and caregivers can see what was completed and what was missed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Reminders by telephone</h2>
<p>
  Not every house has a tablet in it, and not everybody wants one. Remindly can
  telephone the care receiver instead and say the reminder down the line. An
  ordinary landline or mobile is enough — there is nothing to install, no
  account to make, and no screen to read.
</p>
<p>
  The call says who is calling and what the reminder is, and then waits for a
  single keypress — one of three, and never more than one:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Press 1</strong> — done. It is recorded exactly as pressing <strong>Done</strong> on the screen would be.</li>
  <li><strong>Press 2</strong> — remind me again shortly, and Remindly rings back.</li>
  <li><strong>Press 9</strong> — stop the reminder calls. They stop from that moment.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Nobody is telephoned without agreeing to it first. The first call does nothing
  but ask: it says who arranged the reminders, promises the calls will never ask
  for personal details, and starts the reminders only if the person presses 1.
  Press 9 instead and there is no second call.
</p>
<p>
  Pressing 9 during any later call stops the reminder calls too. A caregiver can
  ask again afterwards — that request is itself a call, and there is a limit on
  how many can be made in a day — but asking is all they can do. Only the person
  on the telephone can start the reminders again, by pressing 1 on a call of
  their own.
</p>
<p>
  Calls are made between 8am and 9pm at the care receiver's end. A reminder due
  outside those hours is not telephoned at all; it still appears on the screen
  if they have one.
</p>
<p>
  If a call goes unanswered Remindly rings again, up to three times — fewer if
  9pm arrives first. Whether a caregiver hears about it depends on the reminder:
  mark one as a reminder where <strong>being late matters</strong> and the first
  unanswered call is reported straight away. For every other reminder the
  ordinary missed-reminder notice applies instead, an hour after it was due, and
  goes only to caregivers who have that category switched on — medication is on
  by default, hydration and routine are not.
</p>
<div class="note">
  <p>
    <strong>What the call can and cannot tell you.</strong> Pressing 1 records
    that the reminder was acknowledged on the telephone. Like the Done button on
    the screen, it does not tell anybody that a tablet was swallowed. It is
    worth knowing which of the two you are reading.
  </p>
  <p>
    A phone number has to be entered and confirmed before any reminder call is
    made.
  </p>
</div>

<h2>Keeping caregivers informed</h2>
<p>
  You don't have to open the app to know how things are going. When a reminder is
  completed — or missed — Remindly tells the caregivers linked to that care receiver,
  both in the app and by email.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Completed and missed</strong> — a message goes out when a reminder is marked done, and again if one is missed.</li>
  <li><strong>Your choice of categories</strong> — each caregiver picks which kinds of reminders notify them (medication, hydration, or routine) from their profile. Medication is on by default; hydration and routine happen many times a day, so they are off unless you turn them on.</li>
  <li><strong>In the app and by email</strong> — alerts appear on your dashboard and arrive in your inbox.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Tasks</h2>
<p>
  Tasks help caregivers coordinate care. Unlike reminders, which repeat, a task is
  a specific thing to be done once or on a schedule.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Assignment</strong> — give a task to a specific caregiver, or leave it open.</li>
  <li><strong>Scheduling</strong> — set a due date and time.</li>
  <li><strong>Priority</strong> — mark a task low, medium, or high.</li>
  <li><strong>Comments</strong> — add notes and updates as it progresses.</li>
  <li><strong>Visibility</strong> — choose whether the care receiver can see a given task.</li>
  <li><strong>Status</strong> — track pending, in progress, and completed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Connecting — and ending a connection</h2>
<p>
  A caregiver and a care receiver are linked with a one-time pairing code (the videos
  below walk through it). Once linked, the caregiver can set up reminders and see
  how the care receiver is doing.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Pairing</strong> — the care receiver generates a code and shares it; the caregiver enters it to connect.</li>
  <li><strong>Ending it, from either side</strong> — a care receiver can end a caregiver's access at any time with <strong>Remove Access</strong> on their dashboard, and a caregiver can remove themselves from a care receiver. Access ends right away.</li>
</ul>

